/* Common Module — Nineteen Eighty-Four. Texts and Human Experiences.
   ============================================================================
   Two rules govern every question in this file.

   1. It must require reasoning from a specific text to a specific EFFECT (§6.1).
      "What is a metaphor?" is memorisation. "Which effect does the clause inversion
      produce on the reader's sense of agency?" is analysis. Definition-recall
      questions are the fastest way to make a study app feel like a flashcard deck
      wearing a costume, and students see through them in ten minutes.

   2. Where the choices are technique names, NO distractor may be a technique
      genuinely present in the quote (§9.7b). A quote contains metaphor AND
      alliteration AND a triadic structure simultaneously, so "which technique is used
      here?" is not a well-formed question — a student who picks a technique that IS
      there and gets marked wrong will stop trusting the app, and they will be right.
      validate.js cross-checks every distractor against the quote's `techniques` array.

   Distractors are written at comparable length and comparable jargon density to the
   key, because in English the sophisticated-sounding option is almost always the
   intended answer when options are written quickly (§9.7a). Two automated checks
   police this: longest-option scoring and jargon-weighted scoring.

   `a` is always 0 here; Bank.shuffleChoices remaps it at draw time.
   ============================================================================ */
window.EN = window.EN || {};
EN.DATA = EN.DATA || {};

EN.DATA.qCommon = [

  /* ── techniques, reasoned from effect ───────────────────────── */
  { id:"cm-001", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Techniques", diff:2,
    stem:"“Who controls the past controls the future: who controls the present controls the past.” — Party slogan, Part 1, Ch. 3",
    mark:"who controls the present controls the past",
    q:"The clause order inverts across the colon. What does that structure contribute that a plain statement of the same idea would not?",
    choices:["It closes the loop it describes, so the sentence offers no position outside itself from which to check it",
             "It creates a rhythmic parallel that makes the slogan easier for citizens to memorise and chant",
             "It establishes a causal sequence in which each condition follows logically from the previous one",
             "It separates two distinct Party policies so that each can be evaluated on its individual merits"],
    a:0,
    why:"Chiasmus reverses the terms across the pivot, so 'past' and 'present' each occupy both positions. The sentence is circular by construction, which means there is no external vantage point — that formal closure IS the argument about power over history. Memorability is real but incidental; a sequence and a separation are both the opposite of what the inversion does." },

  { id:"cm-002", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Techniques", diff:2,
    stem:"“If you want a picture of the future, imagine a boot stamping on a human face — for ever.” — O'Brien, Part 3, Ch. 3",
    mark:"for ever",
    q:"'A boot' and 'a human face' are both synecdoche. What is the effect of using them rather than naming a soldier and a prisoner?",
    choices:["It strips the image of particular people, so oppression reads as a mechanism rather than an encounter",
             "It intensifies the reader's sympathy by making the victim someone they might personally recognise, which is the effect a reader is most likely to remember from the image",
             "It conceals O'Brien's own involvement so that responsibility for the violence remains unassigned",
             "It generalises the violence across history so the reader understands it as a recurring pattern, so that the reader recognises the Party's methods as continuous with earlier tyrannies"],
    a:0,
    why:"Naming a soldier and a prisoner would give the image two people and therefore a relationship. Reducing both to body parts removes agency from both sides and leaves a process — which is exactly O'Brien's claim, that power is its own end and needs no participants with motives. Sympathy would require the particularity synecdoche removes." },

  { id:"cm-003", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Techniques", diff:3,
    stem:"“WAR IS PEACE. FREEDOM IS SLAVERY. IGNORANCE IS STRENGTH.” — Party slogans, Part 1, Ch. 1",
    mark:"FREEDOM IS SLAVERY",
    q:"Each slogan uses the verb 'is' rather than a comparative or a conditional. Why does that grammatical choice matter?",
    choices:["'Is' asserts identity and admits no degree, so there is nothing in the sentence to negotiate with",
             "'Is' places the slogans in the present tense, which makes the Party's claims feel immediate and urgent",
             "'Is' avoids naming an agent, so the reader cannot identify who is responsible for the assertion",
             "'Is' keeps each slogan short enough to be printed legibly on a poster at a considerable distance"],
    a:0,
    why:"A comparative ('war is more peaceful than peace') or a conditional ('war would be peace if…') both leave room for an argument. A bare copula does not: it states an equivalence that can only be accepted or rejected whole. That is what makes the slogans training in doublethink rather than persuasion — they are unanswerable rather than convincing." },

  { id:"cm-004", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Techniques", diff:2,
    stem:"“It was a bright cold day in April, and the clocks were striking thirteen.” — Part 1, Ch. 1",
    mark:"striking thirteen",
    q:"The first clause could open almost any English novel. What does Orwell achieve by leaving it intact and altering only the last two words?",
    choices:["He establishes that this world is the reader's own with one adjustment, which is more unsettling than an alien setting",
             "He signals the novel's genre to the reader immediately so that expectations of realism are set aside",
             "He introduces the motif of timekeeping that will structure Winston's working day at the Ministry",
             "He contrasts the pleasantness of the weather with the harshness of the regime that governs it, so that the reader registers the regime as an intrusion upon an otherwise ordinary world"],
    a:0,
    why:"An overtly strange opening would let the reader file the book as fantasy. Orwell's method throughout is minimal alteration to a recognisable England, so the reader has no distance to retreat into. The weather-versus-regime reading treats 'bright cold' as ironic decoration and misses that the sentence's power is in how little was changed." },

  { id:"cm-005", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Techniques", diff:3,
    stem:"“Every record has been destroyed or falsified, every book rewritten, every picture has been repainted.” — Winston, Part 1, Ch. 7",
    mark:"every book rewritten",
    q:"Every verb here is passive. What does that do to Winston's account of the Party's work?",
    choices:["It removes the agent, so the erasure reads as weather rather than as policy — and Winston performs it daily",
             "It emphasises the sheer scale of the falsification by listing three distinct categories of record",
             "It creates a formal, documentary register appropriate to Winston's professional training as an archivist",
             "It places the emphasis on the objects destroyed rather than on the officials who ordered it"],
    a:0,
    why:"The passive deletes the subject. Winston, who spends his working life doing the destroying, cannot find anyone to put in the sentence — which is the chapter's most damning observation about complicity. The scale is conveyed by the anaphora, not the passive; and 'emphasis on the objects' describes the same feature without saying why it matters." },

  { id:"cm-006", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Techniques", diff:2,
    stem:"“Thoughtcrime does not entail death: thoughtcrime IS death.” — Winston's diary, Part 1, Ch. 2",
    mark:"IS death",
    q:"The colon replaces a causal relation with an equivalence. What follows from that for Winston?",
    choices:["There is no interval between the thought and the punishment, so there is nothing left to negotiate in",
             "The punishment is presented as more severe than Winston had originally understood it to be, making the punishment sound heavier than any sentence the courts could actually impose",
             "Winston reveals that he has already been sentenced and is writing from a condemned cell, which explains the diary's urgency and the retrospective framing of the entries around it",
             "The Party's laws are shown to be applied inconsistently depending on the individual case"],
    a:0,
    why:"'Entail' implies a sequence — offence, then consequence — and a sequence has a gap in it where appeal, concealment or escape could live. Equivalence removes the gap. The capitalised 'IS' borrows the slogans' own grammar, which is Winston reasoning in Party syntax even while rebelling." },

  { id:"cm-007", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Techniques", diff:3,
    stem:"“Always eyes watching you and the voice enveloping you. Asleep or awake, working or eating, indoors or out of doors.” — Part 1, Ch. 1",
    mark:"Asleep or awake, working or eating, indoors or out of doors",
    q:"The second sentence has no main verb. How does that support what the sentence is describing?",
    choices:["Without a verb the construction has no natural stopping point, so the syntax enacts inescapability",
             "The omission of the verb creates a clipped, official tone appropriate to Party announcements, and officialese is the register Winston has been trained to produce at the Ministry all day",
             "It shifts the emphasis from actions to states of being, which are harder for the Party to monitor, and states of being are precisely what the Thought Police find hardest to detect",
             "It compresses a long description into a short space, keeping the novel's opening chapter brisk, which matters in a first chapter that has a great deal of exposition to deliver"],
    a:0,
    why:"Three pairs of opposites exhaust the possible conditions of a life, and a verbless fragment cannot resolve — there is no predicate to complete and release the reader. Form and content coincide: a sentence you cannot get out of, about a situation you cannot get out of." },

  { id:"cm-008", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Techniques", diff:3,
    stem:"“But it was all right, everything was all right, the struggle was finished.” — Part 3, Ch. 6",
    mark:"everything was all right",
    q:"This is free indirect discourse rather than direct speech. What does that specifically allow Orwell to do here?",
    choices:["It puts Winston's relief and the reader's horror in the same clause, with no narrator to sort them out",
             "It confirms that Winston is speaking aloud, which shows the conversion has become public as well as private",
             "It distances the reader from Winston so that his defeat can be judged rather than experienced",
             "It provides an objective narratorial verdict on whether Winston's situation has in fact improved"],
    a:0,
    why:"Direct speech would attribute the words and let the reader dismiss them as Winston's. A narratorial statement would tell the reader what to think. Free indirect discourse does neither: the sentence is simultaneously his consolation and Orwell's indictment, and the reader has to hold both without help." },

  { id:"cm-009", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Techniques", diff:2,
    stem:"“He loved Big Brother.” — the last sentence of the narrative, Part 3, Ch. 6",
    mark:"loved",
    q:"Orwell chooses 'loved' rather than 'obeyed' or 'feared'. What does the verb choice establish about the Party's aims?",
    choices:["It shows the Party requires interior consent, not compliance — obedience would leave a private self intact",
             "It suggests Winston has been physically broken to the point of no longer distinguishing his emotions",
             "It implies the Party has succeeded in eliminating all emotion from the population except loyalty",
             "It confirms that Winston's earlier hatred of Big Brother was always a form of suppressed devotion"],
    a:0,
    why:"A regime that wanted obedience could have shot him in Part One. The whole apparatus of Part Three exists because compliance leaves the 'few cubic centimetres inside your skull' untouched, and the Party will not accept that. Love, not submission, is the only outcome that closes the gap." },

  { id:"cm-010", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Techniques", diff:4,
    stem:"“Do it to Julia! Do it to Julia! Not me! Julia! I don't care what you do to her.” — Winston, Part 3, Ch. 5",
    mark:"Do it to Julia!",
    q:"Winston's syntax collapses into fragments here. What is the relationship between that and what Room 101 is for?",
    choices:["The grammar breaks as the self does, and the sentence extracted is one he cannot afterwards respect himself for",
             "The fragments indicate physical pain preventing him from completing longer grammatical structures, the interrogators having deliberately withheld relief until his speech gave way completely",
             "The repetition shows Winston attempting to persuade O'Brien through insistent rhetorical emphasis",
             "The broken syntax marks the moment Winston finally begins to speak in authentic Newspeak, the Party's own language having finally displaced the syntax he used in his diary entries"],
    a:0,
    why:"Room 101 extracts nothing the Party needs to know — it already knows everything. What it extracts is a sentence that makes self-respect impossible afterwards, and the syntactic collapse is the evidence that the extraction has reached something below language. Pain alone would produce noise, not this specific betrayal." },

  { id:"cm-011", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Techniques", diff:3,
    stem:"“Orthodoxy means not thinking — not needing to think. Orthodoxy is unconsciousness.” — Syme, Part 1, Ch. 5",
    mark:"not needing to think",
    q:"Syme corrects himself immediately after the dash. How does the revised version differ in what it claims?",
    choices:["It reframes orthodoxy as a burden removed rather than one carried, which explains why it holds",
             "It softens the claim so that Syme's dangerous position becomes more defensible if overheard",
             "It clarifies that orthodoxy is involuntary, which absolves individual citizens of responsibility",
             "It introduces a distinction between Party members and proles in their capacity for thought"],
    a:0,
    why:"'Not thinking' describes an effort of suppression, which would be exhausting and therefore unstable. 'Not needing to think' describes relief, which is sustainable indefinitely. The self-correction sharpens the analysis, and Orwell's point is that orthodoxy survives because it is comfortable rather than because it is enforced." },

  { id:"cm-012", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Techniques", diff:3,
    stem:"“You had to live — did live, from habit that became instinct — in the assumption that every sound you made was overheard.” — Part 1, Ch. 1",
    mark:"from habit that became instinct",
    q:"What does the parenthetical correction 'did live' catch the sentence doing?",
    choices:["Downgrading obedience from something achieved to something automatic, which is surveillance's real success",
             "Confirming that the assumption is factually correct rather than merely a widespread fear, so the sentence functions as a factual report on the telescreen network rather than a feeling",
             "Emphasising the duration of the surveillance regime across Winston's entire adult lifetime",
             "Distinguishing Winston's individual experience from the general condition of Party members"],
    a:0,
    why:"'Had to live' implies compulsion and therefore a self being compelled. The correction to 'did live' removes the compulsion and leaves the behaviour — habit hardened into instinct. Surveillance has not made obedience compulsory; it has made it unremarkable, which requires no telescreens at all." },

  { id:"cm-013", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Techniques", diff:2,
    stem:"“Sanity is not statistical.” — Winston, Part 2, Ch. 9",
    mark:"not statistical",
    q:"Which reading of this four-word sentence is best supported by the novel as a whole?",
    choices:["It stakes everything on one person being able to be right against everyone, which Part Three tests to destruction",
             "It argues that the Party's manipulation of statistics is the primary mechanism of its control",
             "It asserts that mental illness cannot be diagnosed by measuring deviation from a population norm",
             "It shows Winston beginning to think in the abstract, mathematical register of Newspeak"],
    a:0,
    why:"Winston needs truth to be independent of consensus, because consensus is entirely the Party's. The sentence is therefore his whole position compressed. Part Three's business is O'Brien demonstrating that a mind alone cannot hold a fact — which is why the line reads as a claim under test rather than a conclusion." },

  { id:"cm-014", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Techniques", diff:3,
    stem:"“Freedom is the freedom to say that two plus two make four. If that is granted, all else follows.” — Winston's diary, Part 1, Ch. 7",
    mark:"two plus two make four",
    q:"Why does Winston choose arithmetic rather than a political right as his example of freedom?",
    choices:["It is the smallest claim he can imagine defending, which is why losing even this is the novel's final defeat",
             "Arithmetic is the one subject the Ministry of Truth has no department capable of falsifying, since a Ministry organised around records and history has no jurisdiction over mathematics",
             "It demonstrates that Winston's education has been technical rather than political or literary, his years at the Ministry having trained him in records and figures rather than in politics",
             "Mathematical truths are the only kind the proles would be able to understand and rally around"],
    a:0,
    why:"He is not making a large claim but the minimum viable one — a floor beneath which nothing could be conceded. The Ministry of Love then takes it, and by Part Three he writes 'two and two make five' in the dust while idle. The line functions as a hostage note, and the novel collects." },

  { id:"cm-015", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Techniques", diff:3,
    stem:"“The past was erased, the erasure was forgotten, the lie became truth.” — Part 1, Ch. 4",
    mark:"the erasure was forgotten",
    q:"Which of the three clauses is load-bearing, and why?",
    choices:["The second — forgetting the forgetting is what makes the lie durable rather than merely current",
             "The first — without physical destruction of records the process could always be reversed, since a record that still exists can always be produced again by someone who kept it",
             "The third — once the lie is accepted as truth no further Party intervention is required, after which the Party's work is finished and no further intervention is required",
             "All three equally, since the asyndeton presents them as steps of identical importance, so that no single stage of the process can be identified as the point of no return"],
    a:0,
    why:"Erasure alone leaves a gap someone might notice; a lie installed over a remembered gap is contestable. It is the disappearance of the memory OF the disappearance that closes the circuit. The asyndeton makes the three look equal, which is precisely the flattening the sentence performs — and reading them as equal is the mistake the form invites." },

  { id:"cm-016", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Techniques", diff:3,
    stem:"“Stones are hard, water is wet, objects unsupported fall towards the earth's centre.” — Winston, Part 3, Ch. 2",
    mark:"objects unsupported fall towards the earth's centre",
    q:"Winston's list escalates from the tactile to a physical law. Why is that escalation a mistake?",
    choices:["It assumes certainty comes in ranks, so O'Brien only has to demolish the top of the hierarchy",
             "It moves from the concrete to the abstract, and abstractions are what Newspeak was designed to remove",
             "It reveals that Winston's scientific education is inadequate to the argument he is attempting",
             "It concedes that his earlier arithmetic example was too trivial to serve as a foundation"],
    a:0,
    why:"By ordering his certainties Winston implies they support one another, with the physical law as the strongest. O'Brien attacks exactly there — reality exists only in the mind — and the whole structure comes down with it. A flat list of unrelated certainties would have been harder to dismantle." },

  { id:"cm-017", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Techniques", diff:2,
    stem:"“A terrible ecstasy of fear and vindictiveness.” — the Two Minutes Hate, Part 1, Ch. 1",
    mark:"ecstasy",
    q:"'Ecstasy' belongs to religious rapture. What is the effect of importing it into a political rally?",
    choices:["It shows the Party supplying the need for transcendence rather than suppressing it",
             "It suggests the participants are insincere and merely performing the required emotions",
             "It indicates that religion has been formally abolished and replaced with state ceremony",
             "It emphasises how frightening the Hate is to a participant who does not believe in it"],
    a:0,
    why:"Suppression would leave a hunger the Party could not control. Supplying it — a scheduled communal rapture with a designated object — is a more complete solution, and Orwell's diction registers the substitution precisely. Insincerity is ruled out by Winston's own admission that joining in was impossible to avoid." },

  { id:"cm-018", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Techniques", diff:3,
    stem:"“The horrible thing about the Two Minutes Hate was not that one was obliged to act a part, but that it was impossible to avoid joining in.” — Part 1, Ch. 1",
    mark:"impossible to avoid joining in",
    q:"Winston distinguishes two things and identifies the worse one. What is the distinction?",
    choices:["Between coercion, which leaves a private self intact, and contagion, which borrows the body itself",
             "Between the Party's official demands and the additional demands imposed by fellow citizens",
             "Between his own reluctant participation and the genuine enthusiasm of Parsons and the others",
             "Between the physical requirement to attend and the psychological requirement to appear loyal"],
    a:0,
    why:"Acting a part preserves an actor — someone inside who knows it is a performance. Being unable to avoid joining in means the reaction is happening in him, not to him. That relocation of the problem from external compulsion to internal collaboration is the novel's central mechanism." },

  { id:"cm-019", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Techniques", diff:2,
    stem:"“The Ministry of Love was the really frightening one. There were no windows in it at all.” — Part 1, Ch. 1",
    mark:"no windows in it at all",
    q:"Orwell withholds the building's interior and offers one architectural detail. Why is that more effective than description?",
    choices:["The detail is verifiable from the street, so the reader supplies the interior themselves",
             "It maintains suspense so that Part Three's revelation of the interior lands with more force",
             "It shows that Winston has never entered the building and can only report what he has heard",
             "It establishes the Ministry's importance by contrasting it with the other three ministries"],
    a:0,
    why:"A described torture chamber is the author's; an unwindowed building is a fact anyone can check, and it obliges the reader to imagine what needs no windows. The reader's own inference is always more frightening than a supplied one, which is why the sentence does the work of a page." },

  { id:"cm-020", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Techniques", diff:4,
    stem:"“He had won the victory over himself.” — Part 3, Ch. 6",
    mark:"victory over himself",
    q:"Whose sentence is this, and how can you tell?",
    choices:["The Party's — the narration has adopted a formula that calls Winston's defeat an achievement",
             "Winston's — the free indirect discourse reports his own private assessment of his progress, since free indirect discourse elsewhere reports his private assessments in just this way",
             "O'Brien's — it repeats verbatim a phrase used during the interrogation in Part Three, so the narration has begun quoting the interrogator rather than reporting on the prisoner",
             "The narrator's — it offers an objective summary of the psychological process just completed"],
    a:0,
    why:"'Victory over himself' is not a description any disinterested narrator would produce for a man destroyed; it is the vocabulary of Party self-criticism. Orwell shows the conversion by letting someone else's idiom take over the narration — the most economical possible demonstration that there is no Winston left to narrate from." },

  /* ── structure and form ────────────────────────────────────── */
  { id:"cm-021", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Form and structure", diff:3,
    stem:"Nineteen Eighty-Four is in three parts: Part One diagnoses the world, Part Two builds a private life, Part Three dismantles both.",
    q:"What does the symmetry between Parts Two and Three contribute?",
    choices:["Part Three destroys Winston's acquisitions roughly in the order he obtained them, making the design punitive",
             "Part Three summarises Part Two so that readers can follow the interrogation's references",
             "The parts are of equal length, which gives the novel a formal balance appropriate to its subject",
             "Part Two's optimism and Part Three's pessimism cancel out, leaving the ending ambiguous, so the reader is left to decide for themselves whether Winston's defeat was inevitable"],
    a:0,
    why:"The room, the paperweight, Charrington, the book, Julia — each is taken away, and the sequencing is not accidental. Reading it as a plot summary or a mood balance misses that the structure itself is an argument: the private life was inventoried as it was assembled." },

  { id:"cm-022", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Form and structure", diff:4,
    stem:"'The Principles of Newspeak' appears as an Appendix after the narrative, written in the past tense by an unnamed later historian.",
    q:"What does the Appendix do to the ending of the narrative?",
    choices:["It implies a reader for whom the Party is over, contradicting the narrative's total defeat",
             "It supplies the linguistic background necessary to understand the Party's slogans and terminology",
             "It confirms Winston's destruction by showing Newspeak was successfully implemented after all",
             "It provides scholarly authority for the novel's political claims about totalitarian language"],
    a:0,
    why:"A historical account of Newspeak requires a historian, a later date, and a regime that can be studied — none of which can exist if the narrative's world persists. The novel's bleakest page and its only hopeful one are the same page, and the hope is carried entirely by a verb tense." },

  { id:"cm-023", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Form and structure", diff:3,
    stem:"The novel is narrated in third person, closely focalised on Winston throughout.",
    q:"What is the consequence of that choice for the reader?",
    choices:["The reader is misled exactly where Winston is, which implicates them in the plot's credulity",
             "The reader retains an objective vantage point from which Winston's errors can be judged",
             "The reader has access to the Party's intentions before Winston discovers them himself",
             "The reader experiences the novel as a documentary account rather than a personal one"],
    a:0,
    why:"Charrington, O'Brien and Goldstein's book are all trusted by the reader because they are trusted by Winston. An omniscient narrator would have let the reader see the trap; the limitation makes the reader's own reading a demonstration of how the Party works, which no amount of exposition could achieve." },

  { id:"cm-024", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Form and structure", diff:4,
    stem:"Goldstein's book, 'The Theory and Practice of Oligarchical Collectivism', is quoted at length, interrupting the plot entirely.",
    q:"What is the structural argument made by that interruption?",
    choices:["The theory arrives, is absorbed, and changes nothing — understanding is not the same as being able to act",
             "The novel pauses to supply exposition the plot could not deliver through dialogue alone, the plot having reached a point where dialogue alone could no longer carry the explanation",
             "It demonstrates that Winston is a serious intellectual rather than merely a disaffected clerk",
             "It marks the midpoint of the novel and divides Part Two into two symmetrical halves, giving the private life of Part Two a formal centre around which the plot can be arranged"],
    a:0,
    why:"Winston gets the full explanation of his world and is arrested pages later, having done nothing with it. The interruption's length is the point: the reader also receives the complete theory and is also unable to use it. That the book may be a Party forgery makes the futility total." },

  { id:"cm-025", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Form and structure", diff:2,
    stem:"Winston's diary entries are dated, written in first person, and addressed to an imagined future reader.",
    q:"Why does the novel's only private first-person voice matter, and what undercuts it?",
    choices:["It is the sole space for an unsupervised self, and the book it is written in was sold by the Thought Police",
             "It shows Winston's literacy, which distinguishes him from the proles and from Party functionaries, which distinguishes him from the proles and from the illiterate Party functionaries",
             "It provides the reader with dates that allow the novel's chronology to be reconstructed exactly, allowing the novel's chronology to be reconstructed from the entries with some precision",
             "It demonstrates that written records survive the Ministry of Truth's attempts to destroy them, which is why Winston believes a future reader might one day be able to recover the truth"],
    a:0,
    why:"Every material condition of Winston's private life — the diary, the pen, the room, the paperweight — is supplied by Charrington. The one place he can be himself is stocked by the state, which is Orwell's most economical statement that there is no outside." },

  { id:"cm-026", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Form and structure", diff:3,
    stem:"The novel opens with clocks striking thirteen and closes with a bullet Winston welcomes.",
    q:"What does the cyclical closure argue?",
    choices:["Nothing has accumulated or been carried forward, and the last act of the narrative is love toward what destroyed him",
             "Winston's death completes the tragic arc and delivers the catharsis the genre requires",
             "The Party's rule will continue unchanged, which the Appendix subsequently confirms",
             "Time in the novel is presented as circular, reflecting the Party's control of the calendar"],
    a:0,
    why:"A circle has no progress in it. Winston ends with less than he started with and welcomes the ending, so the novel denies both tragedy's dignity and rebellion's consolation. The Appendix, notably, does NOT confirm continuation — it implies the opposite." },

  { id:"cm-027", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Form and structure", diff:3,
    stem:"'Oranges and lemons' and the prole washerwoman's ballad recur across all three parts of the novel.",
    q:"What do the songs function as, structurally?",
    choices:["Evidence that a past existed, surviving in the only form that could — something people hum without knowing why",
             "Comic relief that gives the reader respite from the novel's otherwise unbroken bleakness, which the novel badly needs if the reader is to stay with it through three unbroken parts",
             "Proof that the proles retain a cultural memory the Party has been unable to reach or falsify, which the Party has been unable to reach because the songs carry no dateable information",
             "A recurring symbol of Winston's childhood, which he is attempting throughout to reconstruct"],
    a:0,
    why:"Nobody in the novel, including the old prole in the pub, can date or explain either song. They persist precisely because they carry no information worth destroying — an argument about what survives erasure that is much bleaker than intact cultural memory would be." },

  { id:"cm-028", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Form and structure", diff:4,
    stem:"Orwell's original working title was 'The Last Man in Europe'.",
    q:"Which Common Module concern does that title name most directly?",
    choices:["What remains of an individual human experience once the collective has claimed everything",
             "The novel's setting in a recognisably European rather than global political landscape",
             "The extinction of humanity as a species under the pressure of perpetual warfare",
             "Winston's isolation from Julia and from the other members of the Outer Party, which the novel dramatises through his failure to sustain any relationship beyond the affair"],
    a:0,
    why:"The title poses the module's individual-versus-collective question as a countdown, with Winston as the remainder. It also explains the novel's ending: a last man who is converted rather than killed leaves nothing over at all." },

  /* ── module concepts ───────────────────────────────────────── */
  { id:"cm-029", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Module concepts", diff:3,
    stem:"The Common Module rubric asks about anomalies, paradoxes and inconsistencies in human behaviour and motivation.",
    q:"Which of these is the strongest anomaly to write about in Nineteen Eighty-Four?",
    choices:["Winston writes 'if there is hope, it lies in the proles' pages after recording that the Party denies they are human",
             "Winston works at the Ministry of Truth while privately disagreeing with the Party's political programme",
             "The Party maintains a state of permanent war while claiming that its slogans promote peace",
             "O'Brien is highly educated yet devotes his considerable intelligence to the service of the Party"],
    a:0,
    why:"An anomaly has to be an inconsistency inside one person's position, not a contradiction in the world. Winston needs the proles to be fully human for his hope to mean anything, and has absorbed the doctrine that they are not — both beliefs, in one diary, in one chapter. The others are Party hypocrisies or character notes, which the rubric does not ask for." },

  { id:"cm-030", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Module concepts", diff:3,
    stem:"A student writes: 'Orwell explores the universal theme of the human condition and shows how all people desire freedom.'",
    q:"What is the specific weakness of this sentence as a Common Module claim?",
    choices:["It discusses life in general rather than how this text represents experience, which is what the module asks",
             "It fails to mention any of the techniques Orwell uses to construct the novel's dystopian setting, and the Common Module rubric asks specifically for analysis of textual representation",
             "It makes an assertion about all people that could not be supported with textual evidence, since no single text can demonstrate a claim about the desires of the whole human species",
             "It uses the word 'explores' where the question may have asked the student to evaluate instead"],
    a:0,
    why:"The module is about representation — the choices by which a text constructs human experience — not about the truths it allegedly contains. 'Universal theme' and 'the human condition' are the two phrases that most reliably signal a student has slipped from analysing a text to summarising a worldview." },

  { id:"cm-031", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Module concepts", diff:2,
    stem:"The Common Module distinguishes individual from collective human experience.",
    q:"Which moment in Nineteen Eighty-Four puts the two into the sharpest conflict?",
    choices:["The Two Minutes Hate, where Winston finds he cannot avoid joining a reaction he does not hold",
             "Winston's first diary entry, in which he writes 'down with Big Brother' over and over, filling half a page with a single phrase he did not consciously decide to write",
             "The Hate Week rally at which the enemy changes from Eurasia to Eastasia mid-speech, the enemy switching from Eurasia to Eastasia without the crowd breaking its rhythm",
             "Winston's arrest in the room above Charrington's shop with Julia beside him, where the collective physically enters the one room he had established as private space"],
    a:0,
    why:"The Hate is the one scene where the collective operates inside the individual rather than upon him — the reaction is genuinely his, and genuinely not. The diary is the individual alone, the rally is the collective observed, the arrest is the collective acting externally. Only the Hate collapses the distinction the module is built on." },

  { id:"cm-032", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Module concepts", diff:4,
    stem:"“Confession is not betrayal. What you say or do doesn't matter: only feelings matter.” — Julia, Part 2, Ch. 7",
    mark:"only feelings matter",
    q:"How does Part Three answer Julia's distinction?",
    choices:["The Party does not want her words or actions — it wants exactly the feelings she has declared untouchable",
             "The Party proves her right by extracting confessions it already knows to be false and worthless",
             "Julia's own confession under torture is shown to be more complete than Winston's",
             "Both characters discover that the distinction between saying and feeling was never real"],
    a:0,
    why:"Julia draws a line and puts everything valuable on the far side of it. Room 101 crosses precisely that line, which is why it needs personalised terror rather than pain. Her pragmatism is intelligent, well-founded, and aimed at the one thing the Party is actually interested in." },

  { id:"cm-033", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Module concepts", diff:3,
    stem:"“I'm not interested in the next generation, dear. I'm interested in us.” — Julia, Part 2, Ch. 7",
    mark:"I'm interested in us",
    q:"Set against Winston's imagined future reader, what does Julia's presentism amount to?",
    choices:["A rejection of the whole grammar of political sacrifice, and the only rebellion available to someone who disbelieves in history",
             "Evidence that she is politically naive and has not understood the scale of what they oppose, her interest confined to the immediate present rather than to any political programme",
             "A sign that her rebellion is purely sexual and therefore easier for the Party to break",
             "Confirmation that she has been a Thought Police agent from the moment they met, which would explain both her forwardness in the corridor and her fluency about the Party"],
    a:0,
    why:"Winston addresses posterity; Julia has noticed there may not be one, and that a future the Party controls cannot be appealed to. Reading her as naive is the trap — she is broken exactly as thoroughly as Winston, which refutes the idea that his intellectual framing was what doomed him." },

  { id:"cm-034", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Module concepts", diff:3,
    stem:"“If you can feel that staying human is worth while, even when it can't have any result whatever, you've beaten them.” — Winston, Part 2, Ch. 7",
    mark:"even when it can't have any result whatever",
    q:"Why is this the novel's cruellest piece of set-up?",
    choices:["Part Three demonstrates that feeling it is not something Winston can choose to keep",
             "Winston is proved wrong because his resistance does in fact produce a political result",
             "Julia rejects the sentiment immediately, exposing it as self-flattering intellectualism",
             "The Party overhears the statement and uses it to determine the contents of Room 101"],
    a:0,
    why:"The formulation locates victory somewhere consequences cannot reach it, which sounds unassailable. Part Three's answer is not that he was wrong but that the feeling itself can be removed — the one defence he had identified turns out not to be his to keep." },

  { id:"cm-035", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Module concepts", diff:2,
    stem:"“Nothing was your own except the few cubic centimetres inside your skull.” — Part 1, Ch. 2",
    mark:"few cubic centimetres",
    q:"Why is the measurement stated as a volume rather than described as 'your thoughts'?",
    choices:["It reduces privacy to a physical quantity, which makes it something that can be entered",
             "It shows Winston's technical education asserting itself even in his most private reflections",
             "It emphasises how small the space is, intensifying the reader's sense of claustrophobia",
             "It avoids the word 'thought', which in Newspeak has been redefined by the Party"],
    a:0,
    why:"An abstraction cannot be trespassed on; a volume can. The clinical phrasing anticipates Part Three, where the Ministry of Love does exactly that — and it makes 'they can't get inside you' a claim about geography rather than about the soul." },

  /* ── context ───────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  { id:"cm-036", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Context", diff:2,
    stem:"Orwell worked for the BBC Eastern Service from 1941 to 1943, producing propaganda for broadcast to India.",
    q:"How should that fact inform a reading of Winston's job?",
    choices:["Complicity is written from the inside, which is why the novel treats it as ordinary rather than monstrous",
             "The Ministry of Truth is a direct satire of the BBC and its wartime editorial policies, since both institutions produce material for broadcast under the supervision of the state",
             "It shows Orwell had first-hand knowledge of how radio technology enables state surveillance",
             "It explains why the novel's protagonist is a writer rather than a soldier or a politician"],
    a:0,
    why:"Orwell had produced plausible falsehoods for a cause he half-believed in, and Winston's work is drawn from that rather than imagined. The consequence for reading is that the novel refuses to make its collaborator a villain — which is far more uncomfortable than satire of a particular institution." },

  { id:"cm-037", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Context", diff:3,
    stem:"Orwell fought with the POUM militia in Spain and was subsequently hunted by Soviet-aligned communists.",
    q:"What does that experience explain about the Party's ideology in the novel?",
    choices:["It has none beyond power, which is why O'Brien can say the Party is not interested in the good of others",
             "It is specifically a satire of Stalinism, so Big Brother should be read as a portrait of Stalin",
             "It reflects Orwell's disillusionment with the political left and his subsequent move rightward",
             "It explains the novel's setting in a Britain governed by a socialist party called Ingsoc"],
    a:0,
    why:"Being pursued by nominal allies taught Orwell that method matters more than professed programme. The Party is therefore emptied of doctrine — which is what makes it unopposable, and what distinguishes the novel from a satire of one regime that could be read as safely historical." },

  { id:"cm-038", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Context", diff:2,
    stem:"The novel was published in June 1949.",
    q:"What follows from the fact that it is set thirty-five years in its own future?",
    choices:["It is a satire of 1948 rather than a prophecy, so its details are contemporary observations",
             "Orwell expected totalitarian government to be established in Britain within four decades, which is why the novel names a specific year rather than an indefinite future decade",
             "The date allowed him to describe technology that did not yet exist without implausibility, the telescreen and the speakwrite both being extrapolations from equipment he had used",
             "It places the novel outside living memory, which frees it from the obligation of accuracy"],
    a:0,
    why:"Rationing, bomb sites, wartime ministries and BBC propaganda are all 1948, lightly renamed. Treating it as prediction lets a reader mark it wrong and move on; treating it as satire of the present keeps it live, which is the point of setting it barely one lifetime ahead." },

  { id:"cm-039", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Context", diff:3,
    stem:"The enemy switches from Eurasia to Eastasia in the middle of a Hate Week rally, and the crowd adjusts without pause.",
    q:"Which historical mechanism is Orwell drawing on, and what is his claim about populations?",
    choices:["The Nazi–Soviet Pact and Stalinist reversals — that people can be redirected mid-sentence and will remember always having believed the new thing",
             "Wartime censorship — that governments can withhold information from populations indefinitely, which is why the Ministry of Truth employs an entire department to destroy records",
             "The Spanish Civil War — that alliances between political factions are inherently unstable, so that no ally in the novel can be relied upon and every treaty is provisional",
             "The Moscow show trials — that confessions extracted under duress will be publicly believed, and a confession extracted under duress will still be believed if it is published"],
    a:0,
    why:"The scene's horror is the speed, and the retrospective adjustment of memory. Orwell's claim is not that people are deceived over time but that the past belief can be deleted instantly — which is doublethink operating at population scale rather than in an individual." },

  { id:"cm-040", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Context", diff:3,
    stem:"The proles make up eighty-five per cent of the population and are essentially unsupervised.",
    q:"How does that fact complicate Winston's hope in them?",
    choices:["They are free precisely because they threaten nothing, so their freedom cannot become political",
             "They are too numerous for the Thought Police to monitor, which makes revolution inevitable",
             "They have retained an accurate memory of the past that Party members have lost",
             "Their lack of supervision means the Party has already conceded the possibility of dissent"],
    a:0,
    why:"The Party's calculation is that unsupervised irrelevance is cheaper than supervised resentment. Winston's hope requires the proles' freedom to be an oversight; the novel presents it as a policy, and the old man in the pub confirms that nothing usable survives in their memory." },

  { id:"cm-041", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Context", diff:4,
    stem:"Room 101 contains 'the worst thing in the world', which differs for every person.",
    q:"What does that individuation reveal about the Party's final technology?",
    choices:["It is not pain but personalisation — the Party must know you individually to make you betray yourself specifically",
             "It is fear rather than violence, which is cheaper to administer across a large population, and fear administered at scale costs the Party far less than sustained physical violence",
             "It is psychological rather than physical, reflecting the Party's advances in interrogation science",
             "It is arbitrary, so that prisoners can never predict or prepare for what awaits them, which prevents any prisoner from rehearsing resistance in advance of the final interrogation"],
    a:0,
    why:"A universal torture would produce universal compliance and nothing more. What the Party wants from Winston is a specific sentence — 'do it to Julia' — that only his particular attachments can generate, which requires the file, the surveillance, and the years of study. Personalisation is not a refinement of cruelty; it is the whole design." },

  { id:"cm-042", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Context", diff:3,
    stem:"Goldstein's book is quoted at length and is probably a Party forgery.",
    q:"What does that possibility do to the reader's position?",
    choices:["It denies the reader the understanding the book appeared to offer, since the only explanation may be manufactured",
             "It proves the Brotherhood does not exist and that Winston was entrapped from the beginning",
             "It shows the Party is capable of producing sophisticated political theory when required, and a Party able to write its own opposition's manifesto has no need of a real one at all",
             "It suggests O'Brien wrote the book himself, which explains his familiarity with its contents"],
    a:0,
    why:"The reader accepts the book as the novel's explanatory key and then learns it may be written by the thing it explains. Every conclusion drawn from it becomes provisional — which puts the reader in Winston's epistemic position rather than merely describing it." },

  { id:"cm-043", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Context", diff:2,
    stem:"'Doublethink' names the capacity to hold two contradictory beliefs simultaneously and accept both.",
    q:"Why does that relocation matter more than external coercion?",
    choices:["The Party does not need to watch you if you will do the watching, which makes rebellion unreachable",
             "It reduces the resources the Party must spend on the Thought Police and the telescreen network",
             "It shows the population is complicit rather than oppressed, which absolves the Party of blame",
             "It explains how citizens are able to work at the Ministry of Truth without going insane"],
    a:0,
    why:"Coercion has an outside — a coerced person retains a self that disagrees. Doublethink dissolves the disagreement, so there is nothing left to enforce against and nowhere for resistance to be organised from. That is why the novel's climax is a conversion rather than an execution." },

  { id:"cm-044", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Context", diff:3,
    stem:"Syme, who understands Newspeak's purpose precisely, is vaporised. Parsons, a cheerful fool, survives until denounced by his own daughter.",
    q:"What does the contrast establish about the Party's ideal citizen?",
    choices:["Intelligence is a liability and enthusiasm is the qualification, so the regime selects against understanding",
             "Loyalty is rewarded and disloyalty punished, which demonstrates the system works as designed, which demonstrates the internal consistency of a system built entirely on informers",
             "Family denunciation is more effective than surveillance in identifying disloyal Party members",
             "Both men are eventually eliminated, showing that no Party member is genuinely safe, so the difference between them is one of timing rather than of any real security"],
    a:0,
    why:"Syme is orthodox, hardworking and doomed — because a man who can articulate why Newspeak works can also see that it could fail. Parsons is safe until his daughter, not the Party, catches him. The regime does not want believers who understand the belief." },

  /* ── characters ────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  { id:"cm-045", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Characters", diff:3,
    stem:"Winston is thirty-nine, has a varicose ulcer, and is employed to destroy the evidence of what he remembers.",
    q:"Why does Orwell make his protagonist unheroic in these specific ways?",
    choices:["A rebellion that is nostalgic, sexual and largely verbal shows how little the Party actually has to fear",
             "It makes him relatable to ordinary readers who could not identify with a conventional hero, since a conventional hero would be harder for an ordinary reader to identify with",
             "It emphasises the physical deprivation the Party inflicts on Outer Party members, whose rations, housing and medical care the novel describes in consistent and grinding detail",
             "It establishes that his eventual defeat is caused by ill health rather than by the Party, the ulcer and the coughing establishing a physical decline that runs the whole novel"],
    a:0,
    why:"Winston never organises anything, recruits anyone or achieves any material effect; his most decisive act is writing in a diary bought from the police. The Party's overwhelming apparatus is directed at a man who poses no practical threat, which is Orwell's measure of what totalitarianism is actually for." },

  { id:"cm-046", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Characters", diff:4,
    stem:"O'Brien is courteous, genuinely learned, and does not lie to Winston in the Ministry of Love.",
    q:"Why is his honesty worse than deception would be?",
    choices:["It removes the possibility that the Party's position rests on a falsehood Winston could expose",
             "It shows that O'Brien respects Winston, which makes the eventual betrayal more painful",
             "It proves the Party has no need to lie because it controls all available sources of information",
             "It confirms that the Brotherhood never existed and that Winston was entrapped from the start"],
    a:0,
    why:"A liar can be caught out; a regime whose stated aims are exactly its actual aims cannot. O'Brien tells Winston the Party wants power for its own sake and means it, which leaves Winston nothing to hold onto — no hypocrisy, no hidden interest, no gap between claim and practice to lever at." },

  { id:"cm-047", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Characters", diff:2,
    stem:"Big Brother never appears in the novel and may not exist.",
    q:"What is gained by keeping him a face on a poster?",
    choices:["A poster cannot die, so the figurehead outlasts any man who could have occupied the role",
             "It preserves mystery, which increases the population's fear of an unseen authority",
             "It allows Orwell to avoid characterising a dictator he had no direct experience of",
             "It suggests the Party is governed collectively rather than by a single individual"],
    a:0,
    why:"Mortality is the vulnerability of every real tyrant, and an image has none. The novel is precise that this is a design decision rather than an accident — Goldstein's book explains that the Party needs a focus of loyalty that cannot be assassinated or disappointed." },

  { id:"cm-048", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Characters", diff:3,
    stem:"Mr Charrington sells Winston the diary, the coral paperweight and the room above his shop.",
    q:"What does the revelation of his identity establish?",
    choices:["Every object of Winston's private life was issued to him by the state, so there was never an outside",
             "The Thought Police are more numerous and better disguised than Winston had believed, and better placed, since a junk shop in the prole quarter attracts exactly the disaffected",
             "Winston's judgement is poor, which explains why his rebellion was doomed from the beginning, his misjudgement of Charrington being of a piece with his misjudgement of O'Brien",
             "The proles' quarter is under closer surveillance than the Party districts of London"],
    a:0,
    why:"The diary, the pen, the antique glass and the bedroom are the entirety of Winston's non-Party life, and all four came from one counter. His privacy was a stocked shelf, which is a far bleaker proposition than a rebellion that was merely detected." },

  { id:"cm-049", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Characters", diff:3,
    stem:"Parsons is denounced by his own daughter and is proud of it when Winston meets him in the cells.",
    q:"What does Parsons's reaction establish about the Party's requirements?",
    choices:["Its ideal citizen is not a fanatic but a cheerful fool who will not notice a contradiction",
             "Family loyalty has been entirely replaced by loyalty to the Party across all social classes",
             "The Party's surveillance is so complete that even children are trained as informers",
             "Parsons is performing his pride because he knows the cell is under observation"],
    a:0,
    why:"Fanaticism requires belief, which requires the capacity to notice when belief is contradicted. Parsons's pride in his own denunciation costs him no effort at all, which is why he is more useful than Syme and more durable than Winston." },

  { id:"cm-050", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Characters", diff:4,
    stem:"Emmanuel Goldstein is the Party's designated enemy, and the Brotherhood may be entirely fictional.",
    q:"Why is a controlled opposition more useful to the Party than none at all?",
    choices:["It gives disaffection a destination the Party owns, so dissent recruits itself into a trap",
             "It provides a target for the Two Minutes Hate, which maintains the population's loyalty",
             "It justifies the existence of the Thought Police and the resources allocated to them",
             "It allows the Party to blame military and economic failures on external sabotage, which is why Goldstein is blamed for every shortage and every reverse at the front"],
    a:0,
    why:"Winston and Julia seek out the Brotherhood; nobody has to find them. An opposition the Party controls converts the impulse to rebel into a self-reporting mechanism, which is more efficient than any amount of detection. The Hate and the scapegoating are real functions but secondary." },

  /* ── quotes and recall ─────────────────────────────────────── */
  { id:"cm-051", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Quotes", diff:2,
    stem:"O'Brien, Part 3, Ch. 3, on the Party's motives.",
    q:"Complete: “We are not interested in the good of others; we are interested ______.”",
    choices:["solely in power", "chiefly in order", "wholly in obedience", "purely in survival"],
    a:0,
    why:"'Solely in power' — and the semicolon balances the two clauses as equal alternatives, so O'Brien picks the second without embarrassment. The other options would each imply a purpose beyond power, which is exactly what he is denying." },

  { id:"cm-052", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Quotes", diff:2,
    stem:"Winston's diary, Part 1, Ch. 7.",
    q:"Complete: “If there is hope, it lies in the ______.”",
    choices:["proles", "future", "records", "children"],
    a:0,
    why:"'Proles' — and note the conditional 'if' is doing more work than the hope. Winston writes it as a proposition to be tested, and the novel proceeds to test it." },

  { id:"cm-053", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Quotes", diff:3,
    stem:"O'Brien, Part 3, Ch. 2, on the location of reality.",
    q:"Complete: “Reality is not external. Reality exists in the human mind, and ______.”",
    choices:["nowhere else", "nowhere but there", "in no other place", "not beyond it"],
    a:0,
    why:"'Nowhere else' — three flat words closing the sentence off. The other phrasings mean roughly the same and all soften it; part of what makes O'Brien frightening is that his prose never pads." },

  { id:"cm-054", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Quotes", diff:3,
    stem:"Syme, Part 1, Ch. 5, on the purpose of Newspeak.",
    q:"Complete: “In the end we shall make thoughtcrime literally impossible, because there will be ______.”",
    choices:["no words in which to express it", "no minds able to conceive of it",
             "no records left to inspire it", "no freedom in which to attempt it"],
    a:0,
    why:"'No words in which to express it' — the claim is specifically linguistic, which is what the Appendix quietly reports as having failed. The distractors relocate the mechanism to psychology, history or politics, and Syme's whole professional thesis is that language is prior to all three." },

  { id:"cm-055", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Quotes", diff:2,
    stem:"Julia, Part 2, Ch. 7, on what the Party cannot reach.",
    q:"Complete: “They can't get ______.”",
    choices:["inside you", "at your heart", "past your silence", "into your memory"],
    a:0,
    why:"'Inside you' — the plainest words in the novel, which is why the reader remembers them when Part Three disproves them. Each alternative is more literary and would blunt the flat certainty the line needs." },

  { id:"cm-056", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Quotes", diff:3,
    stem:"Part 2, Ch. 10 — Winston and Julia's formula, repeated back by the telescreen.",
    q:"Complete: “______.”  (three words, spoken first by Winston, then Julia, then the telescreen)",
    choices:["We are the dead", "We are already gone", "We have no future", "We are being watched"],
    a:0,
    why:"'We are the dead' — a lovers' formula the Party quotes back as an arrest. The cruellest habit in the novel is not censorship but quotation, and this is the clearest instance of it." },

  { id:"cm-057", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Quotes", diff:3,
    stem:"Winston's thought, Part 1, Ch. 2, on private space.",
    q:"Complete: “Nothing was your own except the few ______ inside your skull.”",
    choices:["cubic centimetres", "square inches", "cubic millimetres", "grams of matter"],
    a:0,
    why:"'Cubic centimetres' — a volume, and a deliberately clinical one. The specificity is what makes privacy something that can be entered, which is the business of Part Three." },

  { id:"cm-058", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Quotes", diff:2,
    stem:"Song from the Chestnut Tree Café, Part 1, Ch. 7 and again in Part 3.",
    q:"Complete: “Under the spreading chestnut tree / I ______ and ______.”",
    choices:["sold you … you sold me", "left you … you left me", "knew you … you knew me", "lost you … you lost me"],
    a:0,
    why:"'I sold you and you sold me' — a mirrored clause that makes betrayal reciprocal and therefore ordinary. Nobody in the jingle is the villain, which is worse than if someone were." },

  { id:"cm-059", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Quotes", diff:4,
    stem:"Winston's thought, Part 1, Ch. 8, on what he actually wants.",
    q:"Complete: “Perhaps one did not want to be loved so much as to be ______.”",
    choices:["understood", "remembered", "believed", "forgiven"],
    a:0,
    why:"'Understood' — and it is the wish O'Brien exploits. Note the hedging: 'perhaps' and 'one' make the thought deniable inside his own head, which is what a lifetime of self-censorship does to syntax." },

  { id:"cm-060", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Quotes", diff:3,
    stem:"Winston's thought, Part 2, Ch. 9, on being alone in a belief.",
    q:"Complete: “Being in a minority, even a minority of ______, did not make you mad.”",
    choices:["one", "two", "a few", "the smallest kind"],
    a:0,
    why:"'One' — reduced to arithmetic, the claim is either heroic or delusional and the novel refuses to say which. The double negative ('did not make you mad') is a man reassuring himself." },

  /* ── techniques, second pass ───────────────────────────────── */
  { id:"cm-061", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Techniques", diff:3,
    stem:"“He was a lonely ghost uttering a truth that nobody would ever hear.” — Part 1, Ch. 2",
    mark:"lonely ghost",
    q:"What does the metaphor do to Winston two hundred pages before Part Three?",
    choices:["It kills him off in advance, so his self-image is a surrender the Party never has to arrange",
             "It emphasises his isolation from Julia, whom he has not yet met at this point in the novel",
             "It foreshadows his physical death at the hands of the Thought Police in the final chapter",
             "It establishes the novel's supernatural register, which returns in Winston's dreams of O'Brien"],
    a:0,
    why:"A ghost is already dead and already unheard, so the metaphor concedes both defeat and futility before any of the plot has happened. Winston's pre-emptive self-classification is one of the novel's quietest arguments about how thoroughly the Party has won." },

  { id:"cm-062", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Techniques", diff:3,
    stem:"“The paperweight was the room he was in, and the coral was Julia's life and his own, fixed in a sort of eternity at the heart of the crystal.” — Part 2, Ch. 4",
    mark:"fixed in a sort of eternity",
    q:"Given that the paperweight is smashed in Part Three, what pattern does this passage belong to?",
    choices:["Every symbol Winston constructs is later broken in front of him, so his interpretations become liabilities",
             "Objects in the novel gain value as they become rarer, which reflects the economy of Airstrip One",
             "Winston's imagination is shown to be more vivid than his political analysis is rigorous",
             "The novel uses antique objects to represent a past the Party has been unable to falsify"],
    a:0,
    why:"Winston builds the meaning himself — the paperweight is not symbolic until he makes it so — and Part Three destroys the object in his presence. The Party does not merely take his things; it takes the readings he attached to them, which is a form of literary criticism as torture." },

  { id:"cm-063", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Techniques", diff:4,
    stem:"“We shall meet in the place where there is no darkness.” — O'Brien, in Winston's dream, Part 2, Ch. 2",
    mark:"no darkness",
    q:"How is this line fulfilled, and what does the fulfilment demonstrate?",
    choices:["The perpetually lit cells of the Ministry of Love — the promise is kept exactly and means its opposite",
             "The final meeting in the Chestnut Tree Café, where Winston sits in daylight after his release, the Café being the one location the novel returns to at both its opening and its close",
             "Winston's execution, described in imagery of light as the bullet enters his brain, the light arriving as the release from consciousness the novel has withheld until then",
             "The reconciliation with Julia in Part Three, in which both admit they betrayed each other"],
    a:0,
    why:"Winston hears a promise of understanding and receives a room where the lights never go off. Nothing about the sentence was false; only Winston's reading was. The novel's most sophisticated cruelty is language that keeps its word." },

  { id:"cm-064", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Techniques", diff:2,
    stem:"“Two and two make five.” — written by Winston in the dust, Part 3, Ch. 4",
    mark:"five",
    q:"Why is this moment worse than his saying it under torture would be?",
    choices:["He writes it voluntarily and idly, so conversion is shown to be complete when it stops requiring force",
             "It contradicts the arithmetic he had earlier identified as the foundation of all freedom, contradicting the arithmetic he had earlier named as the foundation of all freedom",
             "It proves the Party's re-education programme produces measurable and verifiable results, which the Party's re-education programme is designed to produce and to record",
             "It demonstrates that Winston has lost the capacity to perform elementary calculations, the re-education having damaged the faculty rather than merely the belief attached to it"],
    a:0,
    why:"Compliance under pressure implies a self doing the complying. Writing it while bored implies none. Orwell places the arithmetic Winston called freedom itself in the dust, unforced — which is the difference between defeat and conversion." },

  { id:"cm-065", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Techniques", diff:3,
    stem:"“It's a beautiful thing, the destruction of words.” — Syme, Part 1, Ch. 5",
    mark:"beautiful",
    q:"Why is Syme's aesthetic pleasure more frightening than ideological zeal would be?",
    choices:["Newspeak is built lovingly by someone who understands language, so it cannot be dismissed as philistinism",
             "It shows Syme is insincere and is testing Winston's reaction on behalf of the Thought Police",
             "It reveals that the Party recruits its lexicographers from among former writers and poets",
             "It suggests the destruction of words is irreversible, unlike the falsification of records"],
    a:0,
    why:"A regime of vandals could be resisted by anyone who valued language. Syme values language and is the most effective agent of its reduction, which removes the comfort that literacy is itself a defence. His delight is professional and completely genuine." },

  { id:"cm-066", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Techniques", diff:3,
    stem:"“The telescreen received and transmitted simultaneously.” — Part 1, Ch. 1",
    mark:"received and transmitted simultaneously",
    q:"What makes this sentence's plainness effective?",
    choices:["A manual's voice collapses the distinction between broadcast and surveillance without comment",
             "It withholds emotion so the reader can supply their own reaction to the technology, and an unemotional sentence about a surveillance device is more disturbing than an angry one",
             "The technical vocabulary shows Winston's familiarity with the Ministry's equipment, the Ministry's equipment being described elsewhere with the same professional precision",
             "Short declarative sentences establish the novel's documentary and journalistic register"],
    a:0,
    why:"The device is presented as a specification, and the reader has to notice for themselves that the two functions in one box abolish the difference between being informed and being watched. Editorialising would let the reader disagree with a judgement; a specification offers nothing to disagree with." },

  { id:"cm-067", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Techniques", diff:4,
    stem:"“Doublethink means the power of holding two contradictory beliefs in one's mind simultaneously, and accepting both of them.” — Goldstein's book, Part 2, Ch. 9",
    mark:"accepting both of them",
    q:"Which word in the definition carries the real difficulty?",
    choices:["'Accepting' — not confusion or hypocrisy but consent, which places the machinery beyond rebellion's reach",
             "'Simultaneously' — the beliefs must be held at once rather than alternated between, which is what distinguishes doublethink from ordinary inconsistency or self-deception",
             "'Contradictory' — the beliefs must be genuinely incompatible rather than merely different",
             "'Power' — the capacity is presented as a skill to be developed rather than an affliction"],
    a:0,
    why:"Confusion is involuntary and hypocrisy is conscious; both leave a person who could in principle be corrected or exposed. Acceptance means the citizen is not being deceived — they are collaborating in the deception, from the inside, where nothing external can intervene." },

  { id:"cm-068", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Techniques", diff:2,
    stem:"“There will be no curiosity, no enjoyment of the process of life. All competing pleasures will be destroyed.” — O'Brien, Part 3, Ch. 3",
    mark:"no enjoyment of the process of life",
    q:"O'Brien's future is defined almost entirely by removals. What does that reveal?",
    choices:["A politics whose only content is power can describe its aims only as subtractions",
             "The Party intends to reduce the population's expectations to make governing simpler",
             "O'Brien is describing the present rather than the future, since these things are already gone",
             "Pleasure is being redirected toward the Party rather than eliminated altogether"],
    a:0,
    why:"Any programme with a positive goal could describe what it is building. O'Brien can only list what will not exist, because there is nothing after power for power to be for. The negation is not rhetorical flourish — it is the shape of an ideology with no content." },

  { id:"cm-069", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Techniques", diff:3,
    stem:"“They were the last of a race of giants, singing over their tubs.” — Winston on the proles, Part 2, Ch. 10",
    mark:"race of giants",
    q:"What does the collision of registers in this sentence expose about Winston?",
    choices:["He can only see the proles as legend, never as neighbours, so his hope in them is a form of tourism",
             "He recognises their physical strength, which the Party has been unable to diminish",
             "He is quoting Party propaganda about the dignity of labour without realising it",
             "He mourns a working class that has already effectively ceased to exist in Airstrip One"],
    a:0,
    why:"'A race of giants' is mythic; 'over their tubs' is a washhouse. Winston's admiration is real and requires them to be legendary — which is why he never speaks to one usefully, and why the pub scene ends in failure. Idealisation is its own kind of distance." },

  { id:"cm-070", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Techniques", diff:3,
    stem:"“The old man's memory was nothing but a rubbish-heap of details.” — Part 1, Ch. 8",
    mark:"rubbish-heap of details",
    q:"What consolation does this scene deliberately refuse?",
    choices:["That ordinary memory can substitute for records, since forgetting does not require a Ministry",
             "That the proles are capable of political organisation if given accurate information, which is what Winston hopes for when he follows the old man into the public bar",
             "That Winston's own memories are reliable enough to reconstruct the pre-Party past",
             "That alcohol has not destroyed the older generation's capacity for recollection, which the scene establishes in some detail before the old man begins to reminisce at all"],
    a:0,
    why:"Winston goes looking for history in a living witness and gets anecdote — beer measures, boots, a top hat. The Party's falsification is not the only reason the past is unavailable, which makes the loss structural rather than merely political. Nobody needed to erase this." },

  { id:"cm-071", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Techniques", diff:2,
    stem:"“Down with Big Brother, down with Big Brother, down with Big Brother.” — Winston's diary, Part 1, Ch. 1, filling half a page",
    mark:"down with Big Brother",
    q:"What does the content of Winston's first rebellious act indicate?",
    choices:["It has no content beyond its own repetition, which measures how little room is left for thought",
             "It shows he has been planning active resistance for some time before opening the diary, the diary having been bought weeks earlier and hidden until he could bring himself to open it",
             "It demonstrates that Newspeak has already reduced his vocabulary to Party formulas, so that even his most private act is conducted in the only vocabulary now available to him",
             "It reveals his rebellion is emotional rather than political and therefore easily broken, which is why the Party regards emotional rebellion as the easiest kind to dismantle"],
    a:0,
    why:"He sits down to write and produces one phrase, involuntarily, over and over. A rebellion whose whole articulation is a slogan reversed shows that even opposition has to be conducted in the Party's vocabulary — there is no other available." },

  { id:"cm-072", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Techniques", diff:3,
    stem:"“To the future or to the past, to a time when thought is free.” — Winston's diary, Part 1, Ch. 2",
    mark:"to the past",
    q:"Why does Winston toast in both directions?",
    choices:["He cannot tell which way freedom lies, and the Appendix answers with a future long after he stops mattering",
             "He is deliberately obscuring his meaning in case the diary is discovered by the Thought Police",
             "The Party's control of chronology means he genuinely does not know what year it is",
             "He wishes to include both the pre-revolutionary past and the post-Party future in his hope"],
    a:0,
    why:"A toast addressed either way is a man who has lost his orientation, not a man being inclusive. The novel does eventually supply a free reader — the Appendix's historian — but the answer arrives after Winston has been erased, which is precisely the shape of his defeat." },

  { id:"cm-073", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Techniques", diff:4,
    stem:"“He who controls the past, ran the Party slogan, controls the future.” — Part 1, Ch. 3",
    mark:"ran the Party slogan",
    q:"The narration interrupts itself mid-clause to attribute the idea. What does the interruption demonstrate?",
    choices:["The narration cannot state the thought without flagging its source, so Party language has colonised even the sentences describing it",
             "The narrator is distancing themselves from a claim they consider politically unreliable, which is why the attribution is inserted rather than allowed to stand as the narrator's own",
             "Winston is quoting from memory and is uncertain whether he has the wording exactly right, the slogan having been repeated so often that its exact wording has become unstable",
             "Orwell is signalling to the reader that the slogan should be treated as thematically significant"],
    a:0,
    why:"Compare the direct quotation of the same slogan elsewhere: here it arrives inside a sentence that has to stop and label it. The mid-clause attribution shows the thought is unavailable in any other form — the analysis of Party language is conducted in Party language." },

  { id:"cm-074", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Techniques", diff:3,
    stem:"“The Ministry of Truth, which concerned itself with news, entertainment, education and the fine arts.” — Part 1, Ch. 1",
    mark:"Ministry of Truth",
    q:"The list is flat and administrative. Why is that funnier and more chilling than an exposé would be?",
    choices:["Propaganda has no separate department because every department is one, and naming it 'Truth' demonstrates who defines words",
             "It shows the Ministry is inefficiently organised across too many unrelated responsibilities",
             "The neutrality of the list allows Orwell to avoid stating a political position directly",
             "It establishes the enormous scale of the building described in the preceding paragraph, which the novel measures precisely at three hundred metres and thirty floors above London"],
    a:0,
    why:"An exposé would imply concealment. There is none: the remit is published, and the name is not a euphemism but a demonstration. The joke and the threat are the same — the Party is not hiding that it controls truth, it is showing that it can rename it." },

  { id:"cm-075", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Techniques", diff:3,
    stem:"“Tragedy, he perceived, belonged to the ancient time, to a time when there was still privacy, love and friendship.” — Part 1, Ch. 3",
    mark:"belonged to the ancient time",
    q:"The novel then proceeds to be a tragedy. What does that contradiction argue?",
    choices:["The private self has persisted after all, which is the strongest claim the novel makes for it",
             "Winston is mistaken about his own era, which shows how thoroughly the Party has misinformed him",
             "The novel is not in fact a tragedy, since Winston survives and is released at the end",
             "Orwell is signalling that generic conventions have no application to a totalitarian setting"],
    a:0,
    why:"Winston argues that tragedy needs something to lose and that the Party has removed the preconditions. The novel disproves him by being one, which means privacy, love and friendship were still available to be destroyed. It is the closest the book comes to conceding that the Party had real work to do." },

  { id:"cm-076", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Techniques", diff:2,
    stem:"“What happens to you here is for ever.” — O'Brien, Part 3, Ch. 2",
    mark:"for ever",
    q:"'For ever' recurs across O'Brien's speeches. What is repetition doing in place of?",
    choices:["Argument — insistence rather than logic establishes that there is no outside to appeal to",
             "Threat — the phrase functions as intimidation designed to accelerate Winston's confession, designed to accelerate a confession the interrogators already have in their files",
             "Explanation — it summarises the Party's long-term political and demographic strategy, which Goldstein's book sets out at length in the chapters Winston reads in Part Two",
             "Reassurance — O'Brien is telling Winston that the process will eventually end in peace, the process being described as one that will eventually conclude in a kind of peace"],
    a:0,
    why:"O'Brien never proves that the Party's rule is permanent; he says so, repeatedly, in different contexts. Repetition manufactures the sense of an unbounded present, which is a rhetorical achievement rather than a demonstrated fact — and it works." },

  { id:"cm-077", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Techniques", diff:3,
    stem:"“Newspeak was designed not to extend but to diminish the range of thought.” — Appendix",
    mark:"not to extend but to diminish",
    q:"Which feature of this sentence carries the most weight?",
    choices:["The past tense, which requires a later historian and therefore a regime that has ended",
             "The antithesis, which sets Newspeak against the natural development of languages",
             "The passive voice, which conceals who within the Party made the design decision",
             "The word 'range', which suggests thought is measurable in the way vocabulary is"],
    a:0,
    why:"'Was designed' is doing something the narrative's ending forbids: describing the Party's project as finished and available for study. The antithesis and passive are real features, but only the tense contradicts the novel's last page — and it does so on the novel's last page." },

  { id:"cm-078", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Techniques", diff:3,
    stem:"“He had the sensation of stepping into the dampness of a grave.” — Part 1, Ch. 1, as Winston enters the alcove to begin his diary",
    mark:"dampness of a grave",
    q:"Orwell's foreshadowing here is not subtle. What is achieved by its bluntness?",
    choices:["The plot reads less like suspense than like a sentence being carried out",
             "The reader is prepared for a death that does not in fact occur, creating false expectation",
             "It establishes the novel's gothic register, which returns in the Ministry of Love",
             "It emphasises the physical squalor of Victory Mansions and Outer Party accommodation"],
    a:0,
    why:"Suspense requires uncertainty about outcome. Burying Winston in the sentence where he starts his diary removes it, so the reader's attention shifts from what will happen to how it will be done — which is where Orwell wants it, since the mechanism is the argument." },

  { id:"cm-079", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Techniques", diff:4,
    stem:"“Under the window, a monstrous woman, solid as a Norman pillar.” — Winston on the prole washerwoman, Part 2, Ch. 10",
    mark:"solid as a Norman pillar",
    q:"Why does the simile reach back nine centuries?",
    choices:["It finds something the Party has not rewritten, so endurance rather than action becomes the basis of Winston's hope",
             "It emphasises her physical size, which contrasts with Winston's own frailty and ill health, set against the varicose ulcer and the coughing fits the novel returns to repeatedly",
             "It alludes to England's architectural heritage, which the Party has systematically demolished",
             "It shows Winston's education included the medieval history the Party no longer teaches, which the Ministry of Truth has otherwise removed from the curriculum along with the records"],
    a:0,
    why:"Norman stonework predates every record the Ministry could falsify, and Winston reaches for it because he needs something outside the Party's reach. But a pillar does not act — it stands. Every image he uses for the proles is one of persistence rather than agency, which is why his hope never becomes a plan." },

  { id:"cm-080", mod:"common", text:"1984", topic:"Techniques", diff:3,
    stem:"“Nothing is your own, except your body, and even that is not for long.” — Part 3",
    mark:"even that is not for long",
    q:"How does the placement of the withdrawal affect its force?",
    choices:["It arrives in a subordinate clause, almost casually, which is how the novel's bleakest revisions always come",
             "It concludes the sentence emphatically, which underlines the totality of the Party's claim",
             "It contradicts the earlier claim about the cubic centimetres, revealing Winston's confusion",
             "It anticipates his physical execution, which the final chapter goes on to describe"],
    a:0,
    why:"The one exception Winston had preserved is removed in a trailing clause, as an afterthought. Orwell's habit is to deliver the worst news without emphasis — the flatness is what makes it read as fact rather than as rhetoric, and it is far more effective than a climax would be." }
];
